# Secrets do NOT live in .env files. Every credential and connection detail - the database
# (host, port, name, user, password), the JWT signing key, the Fauxnance key and endpoint, and the
# SMTP login - is read from the TrustMe vault (leapcapstoneteam1-720d03.TM) by the Trade API, the
# executor, the auth service and the Python scripts. See README.md, "Configuration and secrets".
#
# What is left here is not secret, and is only for the two cases the vault cannot cover.

# Only needed if psql isn't on PATH and isn't found under the usual
# C:\Program Files\PostgreSQL\<version>\bin. Export it as an environment variable (the scripts
# read the PSQL_BIN variable, not this file).
PSQL_BIN=

# Read by Application/Infrastructure/Kafka (up.sh and docker-compose.yml), on the Linux box running
# Kafka. Not used by run-local.ps1 on Windows - that script takes the Kafka host as a parameter
# (or prompts for it) and doesn't read this file at all.
# KAFKA_PORT is the one port Kafka listens on, publishes and advertises. Normally leave it: run
# "bash Application/Infrastructure/Kafka/up.sh --port 9092" to change it (up.sh saves the choice
# here). run-local.ps1 tries 29092 and then 9092 by itself; for any other port pass -KafkaPort.
KAFKA_PORT=29092

# The address Kafka hands back to a client outside this container after the initial
# bootstrap connect (its PLAINTEXT advertised listener) - in this setup, that's every
# client there is, since nothing else runs alongside Kafka anymore. Needs to be the Linux
# box's own reachable private IP/hostname (the same value you'll type into run-local.ps1's
# Kafka host prompt on Windows) - normally you never touch this file directly, though:
# Application/Infrastructure/Kafka/up.sh copies this example to .env in the repository root and
# auto-detects this value on first run. Only edit it by hand if that detection fails.
KAFKA_ADVERTISED_HOST=localhost
